.TH BTD 4
.SH NAME
btd \- the Bluetooth server (port "bt")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Ports
.B bt
and
.B btaudio
(for audiod only).
.B /bin/btd
is started at boot by
.BR init (8),
with device rights, so bonded keyboards and mice come back; with no adapter
it ends with status 0.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B btd
brings the controller up and runs the Bluetooth stack (HCI, L2CAP, SMP,
SDP, HID, A2DP, AVRCP, RFCOMM, OBEX).
On a PC the controller is Intel's, a USB device whose packets the USB server
passes (port "bthci",
.BR usb (4));
btd first loads its firmware.
On the Raspberry Pis it is the CYW43455's Bluetooth on a UART, with
Broadcom's patch.
Every step is logged ("bt: ...").
.PP
Bonded keyboards and mice (classic HID, HID over GATT) type into the
console like USB ones
.RB ( CON_INJECT ).
Headphones become an output of the audio server
.RB ( audiod (4)),
whose writes come on the port
.BR btaudio .
Files offered by a device (OBEX Object Push) are taken only if the user says
so, into the desktop user's ~/Downloads.
Addresses are 6 bytes, least significant first.
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B BT_STATUS
rbuf <\- bt_status_t: the state (off, starting, on, failed), scanning, the
pairing's step and its 6-digit code, the controller and its firmware, the
last failure. \-> 0.
.TP
.BR BT_SCAN ", " BT_LIST
look for devices, classic and LE, about 10 s; rbuf <\- bt_dev_t[]: those found
by the last scan, and the bonded ones \-> how many.
.TP
.B BT_PAIR
(root) sbuf = bt_dev_t: connect, pair, bond; BT_STATUS follows it.
.TP
.B BT_CONFIRM
(root) w[1] = 1: the code shown matches the device's, 0: it does not.
.TP
.B BT_FORGET
(root) sbuf = bt_dev_t: disconnect, delete its keys.
.TP
.B BT_POWER
(root) w[1] = 1 on, 0 off.
.TP
.B BT_CONNECT
sbuf = a bonded classic device: w[1] = 1 connect (headphones: then audiod's
Bluetooth output), 0 disconnect.
.TP
.BR BT_SEND ", " BT_DATA
send a file (bt_send_t: address, size, name), then its bytes, at most
BT_CHUNK each; \-EAGAIN: not ready yet.
.TP
.BR BT_USER ", " BT_ACCEPT
(root: the desktop) whose ~/Downloads takes received files; take (1) or
decline (0) the file a device offers.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/bluetooth.keys
the bonds, and whether Bluetooth is on (root only)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bt (1),
.BR audiod (4),
.BR usb (4)
